  "query_preview": "..\nTo be succinct: Southeast Arizona is poised for \nan active start to the work week. The combination of ample \nmoisture across the region and an upper-level disturbance moving\ninto the area from New Mexico will result in widespread showers\nand thunderstorms...especially east and south of Tucson. These \nstorms will be capable of producing heavy rainfall, with \nlocalized rainfall amounts between 1 and 3 inches. Given how \nsaturated soils are across the area from storms over the past \nweek, there is an increased potential for rainfall runoff to \nproduce flash flooding. Therefore, a Flash Flood Watch is in \neffect for portions of Southeast Arizona (including Graham, \nGreenlee, Cochise and Santa Cruz counties) from noon MST today \nthrough midnight MST tonight. \n\nThe upper pattern this morning continues to be defined with an \nupper trough of low pressure along the Rio Grande in far west\nTexas and an anomalously strong E/W oriented ridge of high \npressure centered over the Colorado Rockies. Things usually get\nactive when features are on the move, and that is what we will\nsee the next few days. That upper-low will nudge into the\neastern portions of Southeast Arizona today as the upper high\ncenter begins to move into WRN KS. The upper-low then then \nthrough northern Arizona Tuesday as the high center tracks into \nOklahoma. Plenty of moisture available east and south of Tucson\ntoday as PWATs are generally hovering between 1.25 and 1.50 \ninches. The area of best lift/dynamics due to deformation of the\nflow aloft as the upper-low moves into our neck of the woods \nwill begin in the Whites and along the southern portions of the \nMogollon Rim that extends into NM early this afternoon and \nspreads across Cochise and into Santa Cruz county as the day \nprogresses. The combination of both these elements will result \nin an increased potential for heavy rainfall rates to the east \nand south of Tucson today. This is supported by the 20/00Z HREF \nwhich shows a widespread neighborhood probability 70-90% for \nstorms to produce in excess of an inch of rain across the \naforementioned areas between 20/21Z and 21/03Z and even a 10% \nprobability for rainfall in excess of 3 inches across Cochise \nand Santa Cruz counties. Per coordination with the Weather \nPrediction Center (WPC), they expanded the Slight Risk for flash\nflooding on their Day 1 Excessive Rainfall Outlook (ERO) today \nto include Santa Cruz county to better support our flash flood \npotential. \n\nIn addition to the rainfall, there is a similar setup as to \nyesterday with the drier airmass residing in west-central New \nMexico getting entrained into the storms as they move off the \nterrain into the Gila River Valley...increasing the likelihood \nfor gusty outflow winds to 40-45 mph/blowing dust to impact the \nGila River Valley and San Simon Valley again today.\n\nOtherwise, the upper-low eventually makes it into our ERN CWA \nby 21/12Z Tuesday morning...then rapidly ejects NNW into north \ncentral AZ Tuesday evening. The probabilities for excessive \nrainfall with both the 20/00Z HREF and REFS remain impressive \nfor heavy rainfall across the eastern half of the forecast area \nTuesday. My concern is that my own experience tells me that if \nan area gets hit hard with additional nocturnal convection \nunder the upper-low like is expected tonight, then Tuesday will\nlikely be a down day as the atmosphere is worked over. In \naddition, I don't like the position of the upper-low being to \nour north during the day. That leaves Southeast Arizona in the\nsubsident wake of the feature. It is possible that there is just\nenough moisture and instability within the lower-levels to \nsupport widespread showers and thunderstorms...but I don't know \nabout the intensity of the downpours. It is possible that in the\nwake another disturbance from Sonora Mexico follows in behind \nand moves across the area to support stronger more intense \nstorms...but that is a big if. It would be just as likely for \nthis feature to move across supporting nocturnal convection \nTuesday night into Wednesday morning. At this time, the WPC \nstill has a large area of Southeast Arizona in the 'slight risk'\non their Day-2 ERO for Tuesday, but there is just too much \nuncertainty for me at this time to message another big day \nTuesday.",
